#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304297 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "80ed79a9-8e39-5c84-9c87-dec266060afc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to First-Order Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "278ca932-6bd2-5409-a12c-59e55f3a91f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304299} {:answer/id #uuid "1fb93bf5-9543-57f8-bc26-332e5dccb902" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304300} {:answer/id #uuid "e93a9665-2338-5665-a5fb-0695e3b625bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304301} {:answer/id #uuid "f246ebc7-45e4-5632-8c1c-03c9ed1a380a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304302} {:answer/id #uuid "85570b73-32ed-53d3-9cac-646d81e759b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304303}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85570b73-32ed-53d3-9cac-646d81e759b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304303} :answer-field/id #uuid "75d3c876-9a73-5d7f-be91-1b4288459661" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304298}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "df42d33c-ab0b-52f4-82a5-1108ad30f682" :question/math-academy-id "q-79330" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=4y.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n- $y={e}^{-4x}$\n- $y={e}^{4x}$\n- $y=4{e}^{x}$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y={e}^{-4x},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-4{e}^{-4x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =4y \\\\ -4{e}^{-4x} & \\overset{?}{=}4{e}^{-4x} \\\\ 0 & \\ne 8{e}^{-4x}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y={e}^{-4x}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y={e}^{4x},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=4{e}^{4x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =4y \\\\ 4{e}^{4x} & =4{e}^{4x}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y={e}^{4x}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=4{e}^{x},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=4{e}^{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =4y \\\\ 4{e}^{x} & \\overset{?}{=}4(4{e}^{x}) \\\\ 4{e}^{x} & \\overset{?}{=}16{e}^{x} \\\\ 0 & \\ne 12{e}^{x}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=4{e}^{x}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}]}
